course: dictionary
lesson: 6280
title: Monsoon: a wind with a season
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe something that comes back at the same time every year. A monsoon is one of these. Read the sentences around it to find the clues. «Sailors timed their voyages across the Indian Ocean to the monsoon, often waiting months in port for the wind to turn.» The sailors waited for the wind to change, so a monsoon must be a wind that changes its direction.
explain: The sentence «In summer moist winds blow from the Indian Ocean onto India, and in winter dry winds blow out from the land» gives you another clue. The wind swaps direction with the seasons. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Wind that changes with the seasons fits. Then try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word and check it still makes sense.
explain: The word can also mean the rainy season that the wind brings. A monsoon season is a time of very heavy rain, and the rain helps farms but can cause floods. Informal speech uses monsoon for any very heavy rain, as in a sentence like «It has been a monsoon all week.» When the clues are not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: A monsoon is a wind in South Asia and nearby places that changes direction with the seasons.
fact: The monsoon is also the season of very heavy rain that this wind brings.
fact: «Monsoon» can be used informally to mean very heavy rain anywhere.
fact: «Sailors timed their voyages» means they chose when to sail, using the wind.
fact: India, Bangladesh and northern Australia have monsoon seasons, and the rain helps farms but can cause floods.
exercise: «Sailors timed their voyages across the Indian Ocean to the monsoon.» What is a monsoon in this sentence? | *A seasonal wind that changes direction | A kind of fish caught in the ocean | A ship with big sails | A tall wave that crashes on the shore
exercise: «In summer moist winds blow from the Indian Ocean onto India.» What do the summer winds bring? | *Wet wind and monsoon rains | Fog every morning | Snow over the hills | Dry wind blowing out from the land
exercise: Ellie says, «It has been a monsoon all week, so I will not walk to school.» What does monsoon mean here? | *Very heavy rain | A kind of boat | A sunny, dry wind | A type of farm animal
test: Why did Kilwa and Mombasa grow rich? | Their sailors never left port | They sat on top of mountains | *Traders sailed with the seasonal monsoon winds | They had the most snow in the world
test: Why can monsoon rain be both good and bad? | It stops all farming for ever | It only brings snow to the land | *It helps farms but can cause floods | It never reaches the land
exam: «In winter, dry winds blow out from the land over India.» Which way do the winter winds blow? | They do not blow at all | *Out from the land | In from the ocean | Up into the sky only
